  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Pages are of 120 lb. Magnani Velata with covers of Twinrocker Simon's Green. Text is set in 10 and 8 pt. Garamond, and printing was done on antique Vandercook letterpresses at Blue Barnhouse and at Asheville BookWorks. Wrapped with twine.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Signed and numbered.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Manuscript translated from Middle High German by Hildegard Stalzer in 1990-91; Hungarian proverbs at the bottom of each page are from Janos Baranyai Decsi's Adagia as translated by Gyula Paczolay.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"This edition of 125 copies was created for and supported by the Library Fellows of the National Museum of Women in the Arts, Washington, D.C."--Colophon</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"This book, inspired by a rare medieval memoir, was written in 1450 by a handmaid to the Queen of Hungary. The story describes the death-defying adventures of two women to assure the crown for the Queen's infant son. This richly illustrated English translation of the memoir unfolds to a 325 inch accordion format."</subfield>
    <subfield code="c">Publisher's description</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Library's copy is number 86 of 125, signed by the author.</subfield>
    <subfield code="5">CLART</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="563" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Pages consist of a single piece of paper folded accordion-style, sewn together with coptic binding. Cover attached by parchment straps in a limp vellum style. Wrapped with twine.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="586" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Winner of the 2008 Library Fellows Award.</subfield>
  </datafield>
